I understand your thinking, and it's a common misconception, but in Dubai, the real estate regulations are very strict and comprehensive. Your scenario, even with your own app for simple rentals like studio rooms, spaces, and partitions, would still require you to be a RERA-licensed agent working under a RERA-licensed brokerage.

Let me explain why:

Why "No Freelancing" Applies to Your Situation
Any Transaction for Commission Requires a License:

The core of real estate activity that RERA regulates is brokering a deal for a commission or fee. Whether you call it a "commission," "referral fee," or any other term, if you are connecting a landlord and a tenant and receiving payment for that service, you are acting as a real estate broker.

RERA's mandate is to protect consumers (both landlords and tenants) and ensure transparency and professionalism in all property transactions. They do this by requiring all individuals involved in brokering deals to be licensed.

Listing Properties is a Regulated Activity:

Even just listing properties on an online platform in Dubai is a regulated activity. Every property advertisement in Dubai, whether online or offline, requires a Trakheesi permit (an advertising permit) from the DLD.

To obtain a Trakheesi permit for a listing, you must be a RERA-licensed agent working for a RERA-licensed brokerage. Your BRN (Broker's Registration Number) and your agency's ORN (Office Registration Number) are linked to these permits.

If you're posting listings without these, you are in violation of DLD/RERA regulations, regardless of the property type (luxury or simple rentals). This is true even if it's "your app."

The "Online Application" Doesn't Bypass Regulations:

Having an online application is a great tool for efficiency, but it doesn't change the underlying legal requirements. The platform itself would still need to comply with DLD regulations for real estate advertising.

If your app facilitates direct connections that lead to a rental agreement where you earn a fee, you are essentially performing the duties of a broker.

No Distinction for "Simple Rentals" or "Part-Time":

RERA regulations apply to all types of real estate transactions, regardless of the property's value (luxury villas vs. studio rooms) or the scale of the transaction (a single rental vs. multiple sales).

There is no "part-time" RERA license or exemption for individuals who have another job. If you are engaging in real estate activities for a fee, you must meet the full licensing requirements.

Legal Consequences of Unlicensed Activity:

Operating without a RERA license and engaging in brokerage activities (including advertising properties or earning commissions) is illegal in Dubai.

Penalties can include significant fines, imprisonment, and deportation for expatriates. Your app could also be shut down.

Any agreements you make or commissions you earn would be legally unenforceable, meaning clients could refuse to pay you, and you would have no legal recourse.

Your Options if You Want to Proceed
Given your desire to deal in simple rentals and potentially leverage an app, here are your paths forward:

Become a Fully Licensed Agent (as discussed previously):

This is the only legal way to earn commission by connecting landlords and tenants in Dubai.

You would need to complete the DREI course, pass the RERA exam, and then be employed by a RERA-licensed real estate brokerage.

Even if you have your own app, you would operate under the umbrella of that brokerage, using their RERA license and complying with all advertising rules. The brokerage would also receive the commission, and you'd get your agreed-upon split.

While you have another job, working as a real estate agent is often flexible, allowing for part-time hours, but you'd still be accountable to your brokerage.

Shift Your App's Model (if you want to avoid licensing):

If you absolutely do not want to go through the RERA licensing process, your app cannot be involved in brokering deals for a fee.

It could potentially operate as a pure listing platform (like a classifieds site for landlords to directly post and tenants to directly find, without you or your app receiving any commission for the connection). However, even these platforms often have to comply with DLD advertising regulations, and users would need to ensure they are dealing with licensed agents or direct landlords.

If you facilitate connections and a transaction happens, and you receive any payment tied to that transaction, you are entering the realm of regulated brokerage activities.

The Bottom Line
The Dubai real estate market, while lucrative, is highly regulated to ensure trust and transparency. RERA's "no freelancing" rule is fundamental. Any individual who actively facilitates a real estate transaction (like finding a tenant for a landlord) and earns a commission for it must be a RERA-licensed agent operating under a RERA-licensed brokerage. Your idea of a simple listing app for smaller rentals doesn't exempt you from these core requirements if you intend to earn commissions from the successful connections.
